proof.text:
Write $G^{\mathrm{et}}_M:e_{\Sigma!}^{\mathrm{et}}M	o p_*^{\mathrm{et}}j_!^{\mathrm{et}}M$ and $G^{\mathrm{proet}}_N$ for the graph sheaf maps, and $o_M:
u_{\overline U}^*j_!^{\mathrm{et}}M\xrightarrow{\sim}j_!^{\mathrm{proet}}
u_U^*M$ for the canonical open comparison. Let $d_{p,F}:
u_T^*p_*^{\mathrm{et}}F	o p_*^{\mathrm{proet}}
u_{\overline U}^*F$ be the underived counit mate of the inverse pullback comparison. Then
$$G^{\mathrm{proet}}_{
u_U^*M}\sigma_M=p_*^{\mathrm{proet}}(o_M)d_{p,j_!^{\mathrm{et}}M}
u_T^*(G^{\mathrm{et}}_M).$$
To check this equality, transpose under $
u_T^*\dashv
u_{T,*}$ and test a generator $(t,m)$ on an etale $W	o T$. The mate $d_p$ sends a section on $\overline U	imes_TW$ to its comparison pullback: this is the underived counit formula, restriction to $W$, followed by the localization identity. The comparison pullback preserves restrictions, zero and the sheaf gluing. The open comparison $o$ is the identity after restriction to the open. Consequently both sides have value $\eta_M(m)$ on the same $\Gamma_t$ and zero on its complement. The classical module comparison identifies values of $
u_U^*M$ on etale test objects with $M$ by its unit. Linearity and the sheafification universal property prove equality for every $M$. Testing here suffices because the maps have been transposed to maps of etale sheaves with source $e_{\Sigma!}^{\mathrm{et}}M$.

We check the derived comparison as a mate, rather than infer it from degree-zero agreement. For a bounded-below complex $P$ on $\overline U_{\mathrm{et}}$, write $Q$ for localization and
$$\gamma_P^{\mathrm{et}}:Q(p_*^{\mathrm{et}}P)	o Rp_*^{\mathrm{et}}QP,\qquad \gamma_{
u^*P}^{\mathrm{proet}}:Q(p_*^{\mathrm{proet}}
u^*P)	o Rp_*^{\mathrm{proet}}Q
u^*P.$$
Let $a:
u_{\overline U}^*p_{\mathrm{et}}^*\simeq p_{\mathrm{proet}}^*
u_T^*$ and let $b_p$ be the specified derived counit mate of $a^{-1}$. The canonical Hom formula of [lem-pc-pre-derived-adjoint-functors-general], with $s=t=1$ and $f=1_{p_*P}$, says that the transpose of $\gamma_P$ is $Q(\varepsilon_P^0)$, the termwise underived counit. Thus the two maps
$$b_{p,QP}
u_T^*(\gamma_P^{\mathrm{et}}),\qquad \gamma_{
u^*P}^{\mathrm{proet}}Q(d_{p,P})$$
have transposes respectively $
u_{\overline U}^*Q(\varepsilon_P^{0,\mathrm{et}})a^{-1}$ and $Q(\varepsilon_{
u^*P}^{0,\mathrm{proet}}p_{\mathrm{proet}}^*(d_{p,P}))$. They are equal by the defining underived mate equation, degree by degree. The derived adjunction bijection gives
$$b_{p,QP}
u_T^*(\gamma_P^{\mathrm{et}})=\gamma_{
u^*P}^{\mathrm{proet}}Q(d_{p,P}).$$
Exact pullbacks and $a$ are applied termwise throughout. This computation uses no preservation of injective resolutions by $
u^*$ and no invertibility of $\gamma$.
